# Line endings.
#
# This machine has `core.autocrlf = true`, so without a rule here git writes
# CRLF into the working tree — and docker-compose BIND-MOUNTS ./server/migrations
# straight into the api container. That combination breaks the migration runner:
# scripts/migrate.ts fingerprints each .sql with a sha256 of its BYTES and
# refuses to start when an already-applied migration's contents have changed.
# A CRLF checkout of an LF-committed file is a different byte string, so the
# guard fires on a file nobody edited, and the API crash-loops with
# "006_run_stats.sql has already been applied but its contents have changed".
#
# It stayed hidden because the running container predated the bind mount and
# read LF copies baked into the image; it surfaced the moment that container was
# recreated. Forcing LF makes the working tree match the blob, so the checksum
# is the same on Windows, in the container, in CI and on the server.
*.sql text eol=lf

# The same fingerprint-the-bytes reasoning applies to anything else whose
# content is hashed or byte-compared rather than merely parsed.
*.sh   text eol=lf
